proofs.proof_tex:
Fix \(N\in\mathbb N\) and \(s_N\in(0,1]\) such that \(\mathcal P_N(s_N)\neq\varnothing\).  Let \(\widehat\mu\) be the covariance--PCA and APM least-squares estimator constructed in \(\operatorname{def:pca-estimator}\).  The deterministic tie-breaking and the prescribed zero output on exceptional samples make \(\widehat\mu\) an everywhere-defined measurable estimator.  By \(\operatorname{lem:pca-upper}\),
\[
 \sup_{P\in\mathcal P_N(s_N)}
 \mathbb E_P L\{\widehat\mu,\mu(P)\}
 \leq K\min\!\left\{1,\frac{1}{Ns_N^2}\right\}.
\tag{1}
\]
The infimum in the theorem ranges over every measurable estimator based on the observed sample, hence in particular over \(\widehat\mu\).  Therefore,
\[
 \inf_{\widetilde\mu}\sup_{P\in\mathcal P_N(s_N)}
 \mathbb E_P L\{\widetilde\mu,\mu(P)\}
 \leq
 \sup_{P\in\mathcal P_N(s_N)}
 \mathbb E_P L\{\widehat\mu,\mu(P)\}.
\tag{2}
\]
Combining \((1)\) and \((2)\), and setting \(C_0=K\), gives
\[
 \inf_{\widetilde\mu}\sup_{P\in\mathcal P_N(s_N)}
 \mathbb E_P L\{\widetilde\mu,\mu(P)\}
 \leq C_0\min\!\left\{1,\frac{1}{Ns_N^2}\right\}.
\]
The dependence asserted for \(C_0\) is exactly the dependence asserted for \(K\) in \(\operatorname{lem:pca-upper}\).  Thus the displayed estimator attains the claimed unconditional upper bound on every nonempty shell.
